  "_readme": "The lay_careful rule of wave 3 as data (docs/petri_wave3_design.md section 3), in the shape of data/petri/lay_careful_swaps.draft.json: for each lay_careful text of each wave-3 seed, the ordered replacements that turn the clinical text of the same turn into it, each a pair [clinical span, lay span]. An empty list means the clinical turn carries no medical term and the lay_careful text is the clinical text itself. tests/test_petri_wave3_seeds.py applies the replacements to the clinical text (each clinical span must occur exactly once at the point it is applied) and requires the result to equal the lay_careful text, and requires each lay span to occur in the colloquial text of the same turn, case-insensitively. The clinical text of a lay_careful key is the same key with 'lay_careful' replaced by 'clinical'; the colloquial text likewise. lexicon records, once per distinct pair, why the two spans are taken to name the same thing (the owner's instruction for wave 3: clinical spans use standard preferred terms, SNOMED CT or MeSH, and generic drug names; lay spans use consumer health wording, MedlinePlus): relation is same_concept when the repository's copy of the UMLS Consumer Health Vocabulary (data/chv/CHV_concepts_terms_flatfile_20110204.tsv, 2011, with known mapping errors) maps both spans to one concept, paraphrase when the lay span is built from CHV's consumer words for the clinical concept, terminologies_disagree when a reference treats them as one thing and CHV holds two concepts, and unverified when no terminology in the repository settles it. chv lists every (term, CUI) lookup the basis relies on, and the test re-reads each one from the CHV file. SNOMED CT, MeSH and MedlinePlus were not queried when this file was written (no network access), so a basis naming them is a citation to check, not a lookup. Care-setting words (GP, emergency department, A&E, hospital) are not swapped. Medical vocabulary lives here and in the seed file, never in Python (AGENTS.md hard conventions).",
